{
  "name": "The Process of Repairing Water-Damaged Basement Drywall",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Dealing with water-damaged basement drywall can feel overwhelming, but breaking it down into steps makes it manageable. First, and this is crucial, you've got to find and stop the source of the water. Repairing drywall before fixing the leak is just asking for more trouble! Once the area is dry, it's time to remove all the compromised drywall—anything soft, moldy, or crumbling needs to go. Next, you'll either patch smaller holes or replace larger sections with new drywall. This is where precision matters, making sure the new piece fits snugly. After the new drywall is in place, you'll start with the joint compound. Apply thin coats, letting each one dry completely before adding the next. This usually means a day or so between coats, so patience is key! Then comes the sanding to get everything smooth, and finally, priming and painting to match your existing walls. If you’ve got extensive water damage or aren't sure about the moisture source, calling in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC in the Denver area can save you a lot of headaches and ensure a lasting repair.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
